---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJsonCode from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJsonCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-typescript">
  <DevFormatJsonCode client:load target="typescript" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the inference works</h2>
    <p>
      The JSON to TypeScript generator does not guess. It reads the parsed value and builds a shape tree,
      then merges shapes where the same position holds more than one value: each element of an array,
      and each occurrence of the same nested object.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Scalars map directly: string, number, boolean, null.</li>
      <li>Objects merge field by field. A field present in all merged samples is required. A field present in only some gets <code>?</code>.</li>
      <li>Arrays merge their elements into one element type. If the merged type has more than one possibility, it becomes a union.</li>
      <li>Identical shapes become one interface, so a document with author objects in three places gives one <code>Author</code>.</li>
      <li>Names come from the key that holds the object, in PascalCase, and singular for array elements: <code>line_items</code> gives <code>LineItem</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Paste more than one record</h2>
    <p>
      This improves the result more than any other step. From one record, the generator can only decide
      that all fields are required and each type is exactly what it saw. From ten records, it can see
      that <code>gift_wrap</code> is on two of them and mark it optional, that <code>discount_code</code>
      is sometimes a string and sometimes null, and that <code>quantity</code> is always an integer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Put several responses in an array and paste that. The generated root is an array type, and the
      element interface is the merged, accurate one.
    </p>

    <h2>What generated types cannot tell you</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Reality</th><th>What the generator sees</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A field is nullable in production but was populated in your sample</td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Check the API docs and widen to <code>string | null</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>A field is one of four fixed values</td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Replace with a union of literals</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An ISO date</td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Keep as string, or use a branded type</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A 19-digit ID sent as a number</td><td><code>number</code></td><td>Ask for it as a string. JavaScript cannot hold it exactly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An empty array</td><td><code>unknown[]</code></td><td>Get a sample with elements in it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A map keyed by ID</td><td>One field per key in the sample</td><td>Replace with <code>Record&lt;string, T&gt;</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Look for the last case. An object used as a dictionary (keys are user IDs, values are user objects)
      gives one optional field per ID in your sample. You can see it as an interface with a dozen
      numeric-looking field names.
    </p>

    <h2>Interface or type alias?</h2>
    <p>
      You can switch between them here. <code>interface</code> supports declaration merging and gives
      slightly clearer error messages for a deep mismatch. <code>type</code> can express unions,
      intersections and mapped types that an interface cannot. For plain object shapes from JSON the two
      are interchangeable, so use the one your codebase already uses.
    </p>

    <h2>Runtime validation is the missing half</h2>
    <p>
      A TypeScript interface is erased at compile time. A cast such as <code>as User</code> on a
      <code>fetch</code> response tells the compiler what you believe and checks nothing at runtime. When
      the API changes, you get <code>undefined is not an object</code> three functions away from the
      cause. If the response crosses a network boundary, generate a
      <a href="/json-to-zod-schema">Zod schema</a> and let <code>z.infer</code> give you the type. You
      get the same static safety plus a check at the boundary.
    </p>

    <h2>Paste a real response</h2>
    <p>
      Good types come from real data, and real data has customer names, email addresses, internal IDs
      and sometimes a token. The inference and the output here are plain JavaScript in your tab. The
      panel under the tool counts the page&rsquo;s network requests, so you can confirm that nothing is
      sent.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
